  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Yemen Community Resilience</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Yemen):  With FY 2024 funds, the activity will strengthen the resistance of communities to trafficking and recruitment-especially of children-into armed groups; the activity will support the reintegration of Children Associated with Armed Forces and Armed Groups (CAAFAG) and former combatants back into local communities through individual assistance and community-based support. Interventions under this Program will address a broad range of community, family, and individual needs, including the conflict resolution and reconciliation; support to education, vocational training, and income generation/livelihood opportunities; and the creation of safe spaces and Mental Health and Psychosocial Support (MHPSS) centers. All interventions will prioritize support to vulnerable population groups including children, adolescents, women, and marginalized groups and will enhance transparency and human rights, with a special emphasis on protection from sexual and gender-based violence (SGBV). Interventions include raising awareness of the CAAFAG issue, discussing how to address this threat through child protection mechanisms, early warning systems, service delivery and referral networks, and stakeholders&apos; partnerships. .The activity will support community structures and community-based/civil society organizations to lead prevention mechanisms and support services for CAAFAG reintegration thereby advancing peacebuilding and reconciliation at the local level. Interventions align with the key objectives of the USG Strategy on Women, Peace and Security (WPS): (i) women are more prepared and increasingly able to participate meaningfully in civic and political leadership, in decision-making processes, and in institutions to promote peace and security; and (ii) women and girls have their rights protected and GBV prevention and response in conflict and crisis contexts is strengthened..Of these funds, $450,000 will go towards the Marla Ruzicka earmark.....</narrative>
  </description>
